<title>USA: U.S. April producer prices fell 0.6 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. April producer prices fell 0.6 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-05-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department monthly producer price index (1982 equals 100):</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:	 Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> -				 Apr    Mar	 Apr97/96</p>
<p> Finished Goods	     -0.6   -0.1		0.8</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	 -0.1    0.4		0.6</p>
<p> -Consumer Foods	    -0.4    0.9		2.4</p>
<p> -Energy Goods		-2.6   -3.4	     -1.3</p>
<p> Finished Goods Index-X  131.6  132.2		n/a</p>
<p>X-Data not seasonally adjusted. N/A-Not Available.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:	 Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Finished Goods		Apr    Mar	 Apr97/96</p>
<p> -Gasoline		    -4.1   -4.0	     -4.1</p>
<p> -Heating Oil		  1.4   -9.9	    -14.2</p>
<p> -Tobacco Products	   0.9    2.5		6.0</p>
<p> -Passenger Cars	    -0.5    0.2	     -0.1</p>
<p> -Capital Equipment	 -0.4    0.3		0.1</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	 -0.3   -0.6	     -0.1</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	 Unch   Unch		0.2</p>
<p> -Manufact Materials	-0.1   -0.1		0.1</p>
<p> -Construction Materials   0.3    0.3		2.9</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:	 Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	  Apr    Mar	 Apr97/96</p>
<p> -Intermed.Energy Goods   -2.0   -4.6	     -2.5</p>
<p> Crude Goods		  -0.9   -6.9	     -4.8</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy-X     -2.3    0.6	     -1.0</p>
<p> -Food/Feedstuffs	    3.3    2.1	     -2.3</p>
<p> -Nonfood Materials	 -4.1  -12.5	     -6.6</p>
<p> -Energy Materials-Y	-5.2  -19.2	     -9.9</p>
<p> -Petroleum		   -0.4  -13.4	    -15.0</p>
<p>X-Excludes crude petroleum. Y-Includes crude petroleum. Note - Tobacco products and petroleum are not seasonally adjusted.  </p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast April producer prices to remain unchanged from March. Excluding food and energy prices, they expected a 0.1 percent increase.</p>
<p>The U.S. April PPI decline of 0.6 percent was the biggest since August 1993 when it fell 0.8 percent, the department said.</p>
<p>The department reported that electronic computer prices at the wholesale level fell 4.1 percent in April, civilian aircraft prices fell two percent and vegetable prices plummeted 20.4 percent.</p>
